push_back(&self, x: T) -> PVec<T>   // takes &self, returns a new vector

CASE 1 - room in the tail (tail.len() < 32), which is 31 of every 32 pushes:
    t = clone of *self.tail (0..31 elements) into a Vec::with_capacity(32); t.push(x)
    return PVec { cnt: cnt+1, shift, root: Rc::clone(&root), tail: Rc::new(t) }
  The tree is untouched and fully shared. Cost: <= 32 clones of T, 2 allocations.

CASE 2 - tail is full (tail.len() == 32): flush it as leaf number m = leaf_cnt().
    leaf = Rc::new(Node::Leaf((*self.tail).clone()))     // the old tail becomes a leaf
    if m >= (1usize << shift) {                           // tree is EXACTLY full -> GROW
        root' = Rc::new(Node::Branch(vec![ Rc::clone(&root), new_path(shift, leaf) ]));
        shift' = shift + 5;
    } else {
        root' = push_tail(shift, &root, m, leaf);
        shift' = shift;
    }
    return PVec { cnt: cnt+1, shift: shift', root: root', tail: Rc::new(vec![x]) }

  Note the growth arm passes `shift` (not shift-5) to new_path: the new sibling of the old
  root must itself be a node at level `shift`. The non-growth arm's inner push passes shift-5.

HELPERS:

    // chain of single-child branches from level s down to the leaf; s==0 yields the leaf itself
    fn new_path(s: u32, leaf: Rc<Node<T>>) -> Rc<Node<T>> {
        if s == 0 { leaf } else { Rc::new(Node::Branch(vec![new_path(s - 5, leaf)])) }
    }

    // insert `leaf` as global leaf number m into the branch `node` at level s. Requires m < (1<<s).
    fn push_tail(s: u32, node: &Node<T>, m: usize, leaf: Rc<Node<T>>) -> Rc<Node<T>> {
        let sub = (m >> (s - 5)) & 31;              // which child of this level covers leaf m
        let mut ch: Vec<Rc<Node<T>>> = node.children().to_vec();   // clones <=32 Rcs
        debug_assert!(sub <= ch.len());             // I8; sub > len is impossible
        if sub == ch.len() {
            ch.push(new_path(s - 5, leaf));         // fresh subtree hanging off this level
        } else {
            debug_assert!(s > 5);                   // provable: at s==5 we always take the arm above
            ch[sub] = push_tail(s - 5, &ch[sub], m, leaf);   // descend into the partial child
        }
        Rc::new(Node::Branch(ch))
    }

PROOF that push_tail's case split is total and that `ch[sub]` is always a Branch:
  At level s with `want` leaves stored and C = 1 << (s-5), I8 gives ch.len() == ceil(want/C).
  I6 gives want < 32*C so sub == floor(want/C) with the `& 31` a no-op.
    - want ≡ 0 (mod C):  ceil = want/C = sub  =>  sub == ch.len()  =>  new_path arm.
    - want !≡ 0 (mod C): ceil = floor+1 = sub+1 => sub == ch.len()-1 => descend arm.
  sub > ch.len() is therefore unreachable. At s == 5 we have C == 1, so want ≡ 0 (mod 1) always
  holds and we ALWAYS take the new_path arm (new_path(0, leaf) == leaf) -- hence the descend arm
  never runs at s == 5 and `ch[sub]` is never a Leaf. The child we descend into is the unique
  partial one, whose relative leaf count is want mod C; since C == 32*C', floor((want mod C)/C')
  == floor(want/C') & 31, so the same lemma re-applies verbatim one level down. QED

PROOF of the growth condition: when the tail is full, m == cnt/32 - 1. Growth fires iff
m >= (1<<shift), i.e. the tree already holds its maximum (1<<shift) leaves. This is equivalent to
Clojure's `(cnt >> 5) > (1 << shift)` but stated directly as the capacity invariant I6, which is
why it is easier to check. After growth m' = m+1 = (1<<shift)+1 which satisfies both I6
(<= 1<<(shift+5)) and I7 (> 1<<shift) for the new shift, so both invariants are restored.

AMORTIZED COST: 31/32 of pushes are Case 1 (O(32) T-clones, 2 allocs). 1/32 are Case 2
(O(32 * depth) Rc clones). Measured 2.29 allocations per push_back at n = 200k and ~45 ns/push
at n = 1M — flat in n, unlike Option B.

input.iteration_algorithm:
CHOSEN: pre-collect the leaf slices once, then walk them. Total cost O(n), and it is the variant
whose O(n)-ness is provable in two lines rather than by an amortization argument.

    pub struct Iter<'a, T> { chunks: Vec<&'a [T]>, ci: usize, ei: usize, left: usize }

    fn collect_leaves<'a, T>(n: &'a Node<T>, out: &mut Vec<&'a [T]>) {
        match n {
            Node::Leaf(v)   => out.push(v),
            Node::Branch(c) => { for ch in c { collect_leaves(ch, out) } }
        }
    }

    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        let mut chunks = Vec::with_capacity(self.leaf_cnt() + 1);
        collect_leaves(&self.root, &mut chunks);          // in-order DFS => leaves in index order
        debug_assert_eq!(chunks.len(), self.leaf_cnt());
        debug_assert!(chunks.iter().all(|c| c.len() == 32));
        if !self.tail.is_empty() { chunks.push(&self.tail); }   // guarantees NO empty chunk exists
        Iter { chunks, ci: 0, ei: 0, left: self.cnt }
    }

    impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
        type Item = &'a T;
        fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
            while self.ci < self.chunks.len() {           // `while`, not `if` -- see pitfalls
                let c = self.chunks[self.ci];
                if self.ei < c.len() {
                    let r = &c[self.ei]; self.ei += 1; self.left -= 1; return Some(r);
                }
                self.ci += 1; self.ei = 0;
            }
            None
        }
        fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) { (self.left, Some(self.left)) }
    }
    impl<'a, T> ExactSizeIterator for Iter<'a, T> {}
    impl<'a, T> IntoIterator for &'a PVec<T> { type Item = &'a T; type IntoIter = Iter<'a, T>;
        fn into_iter(self) -> Iter<'a, T> { self.iter() } }     // enables `for x in &v`

WHY O(n) TOTAL, NOT O(n log n):
  Setup is a single DFS touching each node exactly once. Node count = m + m/32 + m/1024 + ...
  <= 33m/32 <= n/31 + O(log n). Steady state: each next() yields one element after at most one
  chunk advance (no chunk is empty, so a second advance can never be needed in the same call).
  So next() is O(1) worst case and the whole traversal is O(n). No tree walk happens per element.
  Contrast: `(0..v.len()).map(|i| &v[i])` performs shift/5 pointer hops PER ELEMENT -> O(n log n).
  Measured on the prototype (i64, release): chunked iter 1.73 ns/el at n=1M and 2.87 ns/el at n=4M,
  versus 3.81 and 6.06 ns/el for the index loop -- ~2.2x, and the gap widens with each new level.

EMPTY-VECTOR BEHAVIOUR (must be exactly this):
  root = Branch([]) so collect_leaves pushes nothing; tail is empty so it is not pushed;
  chunks.is_empty() => the while loop body never executes => next() returns None on the first call.
  `for x in &v { sum += x }` runs zero times, sum stays 0, the program prints "0". This matches the
  reference for n = 0 and for every negative n (I ran ./test11_executable 0 and -5: both "0\n0").
  size_hint / len() report 0. No allocation of a leaf, no panic, no bounds check reached.

TRADEOFF ACCEPTED: iter() allocates one Vec of m+1 slice references (n/32 pointers; 256 KB at
n = 1M). Alternatives considered and rejected for this task:
  (a) Clojure-style cached chunk: keep `leaf: &[T]` and refresh it via chunk_for(i) whenever
      i & 31 == 0. O(1) extra memory, but formally O((n/32) log n) and the "is the cache stale"
      condition is one more thing to get wrong.
  (b) Explicit path stack of (&[Rc<Node>], usize) advanced like an odometer. Genuinely O(n) with
      O(depth) memory, but correctness rests on an amortization argument plus fiddly carry logic.
  Given the "provable correctness and simplicity" mandate, (a)/(b) are strictly worse trades; the
  6-line DFS is auditable at a glance. Upgrade to (b) only if iterator allocation ever matters.
